{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[G]Dooley was a [C]good ole man He [G]lived below the [D]mill
[G]Dooley had two [C]daughters And a [G][D]forty-gallon [G]still
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[G]One gal watched the [C]boiler The [G]other watched the [D]spout
And [G]mama corked the [C]bottles When ole [G]Dooley [D]fetched 'em [G]out
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[G]Dooley, slippin' up the holler [C]Dooley, try to make a dollar
[G]Dooley, give me a swaller And I'll [D]pay you back [G]someday
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
The revenuers came for him slippin' though the woods
Dooley kept behind 'em all And never lost his goods
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
Dooley was a trader When into town he'd come
Sugar by the bushel And molasses by the drum
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
I remember very well The day ole Dooley died
The women folk looked sorry And the men stood round and cried
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 6}
Now Dooley's on the mountain He lies there all alone
They put a jug beside him And a barrel for his stone
{end_of_verse}
